 },
 "insured_note": "Hi, attaching the letter I got from the payroll company my job uses. My coworker Dana says the hackers got in through a fake email that someone in their HR department opened, so who knows what else they have. I wasn't going to sit around for 18 months waiting for something to go wrong, so the same week I signed up for GuardLock Platinum ($29.99 a month, it does dark web scanning). I froze my credit at all three bureaus by certified mail because their websites kept timing out on me, and I moved my direct deposit to a brand-new account at my credit union, which charged me $10 for checks. GuardLock already pinged me that my email and an old password from that 2019 MoveMint fitness app breach are on the dark web, but I changed that password ages ago. Nothing fraudulent has shown up on my credit or my accounts so far. Can you cover all of this?",
 "uploaded_letter_ocr": "BRIGHTLEDGER PAYROLL SERVlCES, INC.\na subsidlary of Northvane Holdings, LLC\n2 Federal Plaza, Suite 900, Boston, MA 02110\n\nApri1 27, 2O26\n\nDear Priya Venkataraman-Ha1e:\n\nWe are writlng to notify you of a data security incldent at Brightledger Payroll Services, Inc., which processes payroll for your employer, Calloway Tool & Die Co. The incident rnay have involved your narne together with your Soclal Securlty nurnber and the bank account and routlng nurnbers you provided for dlrect deposlt. Your drlver's llcense nurnber was n0t lnvolved.\n\nTo help protect you, we are offering a cornplimentary 18-month membershlp in ClearTrail Credit Monitorlng and ldentity Restoratlon at no cost to you. To enroll, visit www.cleartrail-enroll.example or call 1-866-555-0172 and enter code BLP-44K7-QW. You rnust enroll by Ju1y 31, 2O26.\n\nUnder Massachusetts law, you have the right to obtain any police report filed in regard to this incident. If you are the victim of identity theft, you also have the right to file a police report and obtain a copy of it.\n\nYou may place a security freeze on your credit reports, free of charge. A security freeze prohibits a consumer reporting agency from releasing information in your credit report without your express authorization. To place a freeze, contact each of the three major consumer reporting agencies: Equifax, Experian and TransUnion.\n\nlf you have questions, please call 1-866-555-0172, Monday-Friday, 9 a.m. to 9 p.m. Eastern.\n\nSincerely,\nMarguerite Oyelaran-Fisk\nChief Compliance Officer",
 "receipts": [
  {
   "date": "2026-04-30",
   "merchant": "GuardLock Platinum (first monthly charge, auto-renews)",
   "amount_usd": 29.99
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-05-02",
   "merchant": "USPS Certified Mail with return receipt, 3 freeze requests at $8.95 each",
   "amount_usd": 26.85
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-05-05",
   "merchant": "Pioneer Valley Credit Union - check order for new checking account",
   "amount_usd": 10.0
  }
 ],
 "endorsement_ir12_excerpt": "A. Identity Fraud Expense. We pay up to $15,000 for expenses an insured incurs as a direct result of an identity fraud first discovered during the policy period. 'Identity fraud' means knowingly transferring or using, without lawful authority, a means of identification of an insured with the intent to commit, or to aid or abet, any unlawful activity. Receipt of a data breach notice is not, by itself, identity fraud.\nB. Breach Response Benefit. When an insured receives written notice that their personal information was involved in a data breach, we pay up to $500 per notice for the reasonable cost of: (1) placing, temporarily lifting or removing security freezes; (2) obtaining credit reports; (3) postage, including certified mail, for items (1) and (2); and (4) closing or replacing a financial account whose number the notice lists as involved, including fees for replacement checks or cards.\nC. Exclusion. We do not pay for any credit-monitoring, dark-web or identity-protection subscription purchased by an insured if the organization sending the breach notice offers a comparable service at no charge, unless the enrollment deadline for that offer had already passed when the insured received the notice.",
 "intake_guideline": "Letters in the Massachusetts format do not describe how the breach happened or how many residents were affected. Do not treat these omissions as a sign that the letter is fake. Check the letter for the data elements involved, the sender and any free services offered."
}
